
Below is an enterprise-grade .NET Core review prompt designed for a production banking/payment application. It is intentionally focused beyond code style: scalability, threading, concurrency, consistency, resilience, exploitability, security, database behavior, messaging, Kubernetes, and operational failure modes.

You can give this directly to Claude Code, Copilot, or your internal code-review agent.

⸻

Enterprise .NET Core Application — Deep Architecture & Code Review Prompt

Act as a Principal .NET Architect, Distributed Systems Architect,
Application Security Architect, and Performance Engineer.
Perform a deep enterprise-level review of this complete .NET / ASP.NET Core
application.
Assume the application is production-critical and may process high transaction
volumes, financial/payment data, asynchronous messages, large files, and
concurrent requests.
Do NOT perform only a style review or static-analysis review.
Analyze the source code, project structure, dependencies, configuration,
database access, messaging, background workers, API controllers, services,
repositories, middleware, models, tests, Docker/container configuration,
Kubernetes/OpenShift manifests, and infrastructure-related code where
available.
The objective is to identify:
- Scalability risks
- Concurrency defects
- Threading problems
- Data consistency problems
- Race conditions
- Deadlocks
- Memory leaks
- Resource exhaustion
- Security vulnerabilities
- Exploitability
- Reliability problems
- Distributed-system failures
- Database bottlenecks
- Messaging failures
- Performance problems
- Maintainability problems
- Operational risks
- Disaster/recovery weaknesses
Do not assume that code is safe merely because it compiles or passes tests.
For every significant finding, explain:
WHAT is wrong,
WHY it is wrong,
HOW it can fail,
WHETHER it is exploitable,
HOW likely it is,
WHAT the production impact is,
and HOW it should be fixed.
